{"id": "5b12d35f-df8d-4795-9c13-87f10731308a", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:43:11.485144", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "95b6e35d-6ded-40d1-960f-1533a76b50bf", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "The non-causal leak test gave 0 for past leak — because I perturbed the LAST index (n-1) and looked at indices up to n-1; with kernel length 127, output at index i depends on inputs i-63..i+63. Perturbing index n-1=511 affects outputs i in [511-63, 511+63] = [448, 574]. I looked at range(63, 511) — that includes 448..510, so leak should be nonzero... unless the indexing/alignment of make_noncausal conv isn't what I think. In conv_direct, y[i] = sum_j x[i-j]*k[j], j from 0..m-1, m=127. So y[i] depends on x[i-j] for j=0..126 → x indices i-126..i. That's a causal (right-sided) indexing regardless of kernel symmetry! Symmetric kernel with right-sided alignment = \"symmetric-shape but only past inputs\" → it's actually still causal in terms of support. So the leak was 0, correctly detecting that my alignment was wrong: a non-causal operator requires centering the kernel (output at i uses x[i-(L-1) .. i+(L-1)]).\n\nThis is a genuinely valuable finding for the design: the \"non-causal\" property is about kernel SUPPORT alignment, not kernel symmetry, and the naive \"flip-and-concat + standard conv\" implementation silently produces a causal-support operator with a symmetric shape. This is exactly the kind of implementation confound the unit-check protocol must catch. Great — I'll report this as a concrete repository/environment observation and use it to specify the alignment-centered unit test as a mandatory check (shift by L-1, i.e., \"same\" alignment).\n\nLet me verify by implementing the centered version and confirming nonzero past influence, plus boundary handling (zero-padding vs reflection) sensitivity. Also test at H=1e5 length FFT timing to support feasibility claims.\n\nLet me redo quickly.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": null, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-88ca55f180a9cf64", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-88ca55f180a9cf64", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cat > /tmp/feas/ops2.py <<'PY'\\nimport math, time\\n\\ndef poly(L, a=1.5): return [(i+1.0)**(-a) for i in range(L)]\\ndef sym_half(h): return h[:0:-1] + h            # symmetric SHAPE, length 2L-1\\n\\ndef conv_right(x,k):   # y[i] = sum_j x[i-j] k[j]  -> support strictly in PAST\\n    n,m=len(x),len(k)\\n    return [sum(x[i-j]*k[j] for j in range(m) if 0<=i-j<n) for i in range(n)]\\n\\ndef conv_center(x,k):  # y[i] = sum_j x[i-(m//2)+j] k[j] -> support PAST and FUTURE\\n    n,m=len(x),len(k); c=m//2\\n    return [sum(x[i-c+j]*k[j] for j in range(m) if 0<=i-c+j<n) for i in range(n)]\\n\\ndef boundary_test(kind, n=64, L=9):\\n    x=[0.0]*n; x[0]=1.0                      # impulse at left edge\\n    k=sym_half(poly(L))\\n    y = conv_center(x,k)\\n    return sum(abs(v) for v in y[:L//2]), sum(abs(v) for v in y[L//2+1:])\\n\\nn,L = 256, 33\\nk = sym_half(poly(L))\\nzero=[0.0]*n\\npert_idx=n-1\\nxp=[0.0]*n; xp[pert_idx]=1.0\\nfor name, f in [(\\\"right-aligned (naive)\\\", conv_right), (\\\"center-aligned (true non-causal)\\\", conv_center)]:\\n    y0=f(zero,k); yp=f(xp,k)\\n    past = max(abs(yp[i]-y0[i]) for i in range(pert_idx))          # outputs strictly before the perturbed input\\n    print(f\\\"{name:36s}: influence of input[t=255] on outputs t<255 -> {past:.3e}  {'CAUSAL' if past<1e-12 else 'NON-CAUSAL'}\\\")\\nprint(\\\"kernel length:\\\", len(k), \\\"expected 2L-1 =\\\", 2*L-1, \\\"| center index:\\\", len(k)//2)\\nl,r = boundary_test('x')\\nprint(f\\\"impulse at left edge, center-aligned kernel -> energy left of center {l:.3f} vs right {r:.3f} (edge truncation asymmetry: boundary policy must be declared)\\\")\\n\\n# scaling to evaluation lengths, pure-python FFT path\\nimport cmath\\ndef fft(a, inv=False):\\n    n=len(a)\\n    if n<=1: return a[:]\\n    e=fft(a[0::2],inv); o=fft(a[1::2],inv); out=[0j]*n\\n    for k2 in range(n//2):\\n        w=cmath.exp((2j if inv else -2j)*math.pi*k2/n)*o[k2]\\n        out[k2]=e[k2]+w; out[k2+n//2]=e[k2]-w\\n    return out\\ndef conv_fft(x,k):\\n    n,m=len(x),len(k); N=1<<(n+m-1).bit_length()\\n    X=fft(x+[0.0]*(N-n)); K=fft(k+[0.0]*(N-m))\\n    Y=fft([X[i]*K[i] for i in range(N)], True)\\n    return [Y[i].real/N for i in range(n+m-1)]\\nfor n in (65536, 262144):\\n    t=time.time(); conv_fft([0.0]*n, poly(n//2)); print(f\\\"pure-python FFT conv n={n}: {time.time()-t:.1f}s\\\")\\nPY\\ncd /tmp/feas && timeout 900 python3 ops2.py\", \"timeout_seconds\": 900}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-d57e77e8-6bc1-48a2-bf1e-15d2080196ac", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": null, "kind": "ActionEvent"}
